\section*{Polynomiality of the natural invariant anticanonical index}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The paper claims a polynomial invariant Euler index on divisible anticanonical exponents and a conversion that removes a fixed pseudo-effective error from unbounded effective anticanonical twists. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Consider a holomorphic action by a compact real torus $T$ on a compact complex manifold $F$, with natural differential linearization on $L_F=-K_F$. Define
\[
I_T(m)=\sum_{q\ge0}(-1)^q\dim H^q(F,L_F^m)^T.
\]
OpenAI claims some integer $a>0$ and $P\in\mathbb Q[t]$ with $I_T(m)=P(m)$ for every positive multiple of $a$, and $P(0)=I_T(0)$.
Its independent conversion theorem takes smooth connected projective complex $S$, smoothly semipositive $L=-K_S$ and pseudo-effective Cartier $D$; sections $H^0(S,m_jL-D)\ne0$ for a strictly increasing positive sequence $m_j$ are claimed to imply $H^0(S,kL)\ne0$ for some $k>0$. This conversion statement has no group-action hypothesis.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 068, 2026-09-26): Lefschetz denominators become weighted lattice counts with selected faces deleted, and a polyhedral retraction computes their value at exponent zero. For conversion, a maximal rational base makes section ratios descend to base functions and forces affine horizontal vanishing orders, permitting descent of forms before the fixed error is removed.

